SURAH = "al-Anbiyāʾ"
NUM = 21

BLOCKS = {

104: ("The Rolling and the Return", [
 "The verse's two movements — the rolling up of the sky and the bringing back of the first creation — are not in tension but in sequence. What is folded is then restored, and the restoration is measured against the beginning.",
 "Al-Qushayrī's cross-reference to 39:67, where the heavens are enfolded in God's right Hand, shows the Qur'an using several images for one event, and the variety suggests that no single image is meant to be exhaustive.",
 "Al-Ṭabarī's gloss that sijill means rolled-up pages upon which one writes grounds the comparison in an ordinary act, and the ordinariness is what makes the cosmic scale of the verse imaginable rather than merely vast.",
 "The reading preserved alongside it, that sijill is the name of an angel, is not dismissed. The commentators note that common Arabic words often function also as proper names, citing 2:19c and 2:79c, so the identification has precedent.",
 "The phrase a promise binding upon Us is the verse's theological core. The binding is self-imposed rather than external, and the verse records it without qualification or explanation.",
 "The closing Surely We shall do it is as emphatic as the language permits: an emphatic particle plus the first person, with no condition attached and no contingency allowed.",
 "The verse thus completes the cosmic arc begun at verse 96. The unleashing of Gog and Magog was a sign within the world; the rolling up of the sky is the transformation of the world itself.",
 "What follows turns from the cosmos to a specified group and their inheritance, and the turn is unannounced but prepared by the promise's reliability, which is what the binding language establishes.",
]),

105: ("The Citation and the Inheritance", [
 "The verse's appeal to a written source distinguishes it from the verses around it. The promise is not merely asserted but cited, and the citation is emphatic: We have indeed written.",
 "Al-Qushayrī's note that the Psalms are named at 4:163 and 17:55 establishes al-Zabūr as a recognized scripture, and the recognition is what makes the citation meaningful rather than merely decorative.",
 "Al-Rāzī and al-Ṭabarī record the broader reading on which zabūr, often a synonym for kitāb, refers to all the books of the prophets rather than only to David's Psalms.",
 "Al-Qushayrī and al-Ṭabarī add that this would include the Torah, the Gospel, and the Qur'an, so on that reading the citation appeals to the whole of prophetic scripture at once.",
 "The identification of the Reminder with what is in Heaven, the Mother of the Book at 3:7, 13:39, and 43:4, and as being with God, locates the prior term of the sequence in the Divine presence.",
 "The promise itself — that My righteous servants shall inherit the earth — designates its beneficiaries as servants, which connects them verbally to the worshipful people named at verse 106.",
 "Al-Qushayrī and al-Ṭabarī record the celestial reading of earth as the earth of the Garden, and al-Rāzī supplies the argument: this world is shared by believers and disbelievers alike, while the Garden belongs only to the former.",
 "The argument matters because it shows why the earthly reading seemed inadequate to some — if earth meant this world, the promise would not distinguish the righteous from anyone else.",
 "Al-Rāzī and al-Ṭabarī nonetheless preserve the reading that earth means this world, so the reference remains contested and the contest is recorded rather than settled.",
]),

106: ("The Proclamation and the Worshipful", [
 "The verse does two things at once: it characterizes the preceding passage as a proclamation, and it identifies the audience for whom that proclamation is intended.",
 "The demonstrative in this is general rather than specific, so the whole eschatological sequence from verse 92 onward is what the proclamation comprises, not any single statement within it.",
 "Al-Qushayrī and al-Ṭabarī identify a worshipful people concretely: those who perform the five daily canonical prayers and fast the month of Ramadan, meaning the community of Muhammad.",
 "The concreteness of the identification is what gives the verse its purchase. Worshipfulness is not left abstract but specified through the acts that define the community.",
 "Al-Sulamī records the alternative reading, on which the verse refers to those especially devoted to God who worship neither for the delights of the Garden nor to be free of the Fire.",
 "On that reading they worship out of love for God and pride in being a worshipper of God, so the motive is distinguished from both reward and avoidance.",
 "The distinction is theologically significant because it separates worship grounded in love from worship grounded in desire for Paradise or fear of Hell, and the commentators preserve it as part of the verse's sense.",
 "The two identifications are not mutually exclusive. The community performs the prayers and the fast, and some within it worship out of love, so the range extends from the general body to its most devoted members.",
 "The designation worshipful also connects back to verse 105's righteous servants, so the same group is described from two angles: by what it does, and by what it will inherit.",
]),

107: ("The Mercy and Its Range", [
 "The verse states the purpose of the sending in an exclusive form — not sent except as a mercy — and the exclusion is what gives the statement its force.",
 "Al-Qushayrī's note that raḥmah can mean compassion, kindness, love, and caring establishes the term's breadth, and the breadth is what allows the verse to bear several readings.",
 "The grammatical ambiguity he identifies is substantive: the mercy may be the act of sending, which is God's merciful deed, or it may be the one sent, so that the Prophet is himself the mercy.",
 "The commentators preserve both, and the difference concerns whether mercy here is an action or a person, which is not a trivial distinction.",
 "The further gradation recorded from Ālūsī — that the Prophet is a possessor of mercy, is merciful, or is himself a mercy — forms a scale, and each step is stronger than the last.",
 "Al-Qushayrī notes that Muslims commonly read the verse to mean the Prophet is a mercy because he causes happiness in the two abodes, this world and the Hereafter, so the mercy spans both.",
 "The same note adds that the Law's prohibitions and commandments exist for the purpose of attaining that happiness, which makes the Law instrumental to mercy rather than opposed to it.",
 "That purposive framing is worth marking, since it presents the commandments as means to happiness in both abodes rather than as constraints imposed upon it.",
 "The closing phrase unto the worlds extends the mercy beyond any single community, and the universality is what connects the verse to the sūrah's earlier accounts of Divine deliverance.",
]),

108: ("The Declaration and the Invitation", [
 "The verse opens with a restriction and closes with a question, and the two are what give it its shape. Only this is revealed, and will you therefore submit?",
 "The restriction is emphatic and limits what the Prophet claims for himself. The message is not his own but what was revealed, and the revelation is the sole source.",
 "The content of that revelation is stated in a single clause — that your God is one God — and al-Qushayrī's cross-references to 2:163, 16:22, 22:34, 29:46, and 41:6 show it is the repeated core of the message.",
 "The accumulation of parallels is what gives the declaration its authority. The unity of God is not a local claim but the recurring content of revelation throughout the Qur'an.",
 "The closing question is an invitation rather than a command. The addressees are asked whether they will be submitters, and al-Qushayrī notes that submitters renders muslim, with 2:128c for the term.",
 "The interrogative form matters because it leaves the answer with the hearer. Nothing is predicted and nothing is compelled; the question is put and awaits a response.",
 "The sequence from declaration to question implies an argument the verse does not spell out: because God is one, submission is fitting, and the fittingness is what the question assumes.",
 "The verse's brevity is functional rather than a limitation. The sūrah's concluding verses state their content without development, and the compression is what allows this one to serve as a summary.",
 "It also restates in its simplest form the unity asserted at verse 92, so the theme that opened the passage closes it, and the closing is what the verse provides.",
]),

109: ("The Completed Proclamation", [
 "The verse opens with a condition rather than a prediction. The turning away may occur, and the instruction that follows is for that case, which makes the verse practical rather than merely descriptive.",
 "The instruction is to state that the proclamation has been made to all equally, and al-Qushayrī and al-Rāzī gloss this as meaning no part of the revelation was kept secret from anyone.",
 "The cross-reference to 81:24, where it is said that he does not withhold grudgingly knowledge of the unseen, supports the gloss and establishes the theme elsewhere in the Qur'an.",
 "The denial of concealment is what answers any charge of partiality, and the answer is stated as a fact about what was done rather than as a defence.",
 "Al-Qushayrī notes that equally renders ʿalā sawāʾ, a phrase rendered in a just way at 8:58 in the instruction to withdraw from a people justly, so the phrase carries both equality and justice.",
 "Al-Rāzī records that because of this shared wording some read ʿalā sawāʾ as fair warning of a coming state of war, on which the verse means I have proclaimed to you all in a just way.",
 "The two readings differ substantively — a proclamation without distinction versus a just notification of hostilities — and the commentators preserve both without settling between them.",
 "The second statement disclaims knowledge of timing: the Prophet does not know whether what is promised is near or far, which separates conveying the message from knowing the unseen's schedule.",
 "Al-Qushayrī notes that the limitation of the Prophet's responsibility to conveyance is a common Qur'anic theme, so the disclaimer restates a position asserted repeatedly elsewhere.",
 "Together the two statements define the role precisely: the message has been delivered in full, and its timing is not the messenger's to know, and the definition is what the sūrah's conclusion supplies.",
]),

}
